# Workflow

## Sources of truth, in order

1. **The live Figma file** `ks-MarketingSite` (`IBO39siJYDhiCRtuLWUTW2`), read
   through the Figma MCP. Every geometry, type, color, and exposure value
   comes from a node read at build time. See `40-figma.mdc`.
2. **The code.** The built values live in the token layers, the data modules,
   and the components. When you extend or fix built work, read the code for
   the current values — never a document, never memory.
3. **`docs/reference/`** — the grid mechanics (`grid-engine.md`) and the token
   architecture (`tokens.md`). Law for *how*, never for content values.

There are no spec documents. Design intent is the Figma file; the record of a
build is the code and its comments; the standing decisions are these rules.
Do not create spec, plan, or decision-log documents.

## Standing owner decisions

- Big-bang launch: nothing deploys until every page is complete.
- Light mode only.
- Nav links to pages that do not exist yet 404 until those pages are built.
- No QA or fixture routes. Acceptance and the grid sweep run on the live pages.
  Dev-only sweep hooks mount through a `*-qa` wrapper that production aliases
  to a null stub — no panel, no fixture URL.
- The tick caps at 112px above a 1344 container; the 1344 design renders exact
  at every wider width and the side fields fill the gutters.

## Verification gates

Before you report a change as done:

- `npx tsc --noEmit` — zero errors. `npm run lint` — zero errors or warnings.
- Zero unused imports, variables, or dead code.
- Visual work is verified in the browser at the five anchors (384 · 576 ·
  768 · 960 · 1344) and one arbitrary mid-band width per band, scrollbar
  forced on. Report the measured evidence, not "looks right".
- Layout work under `design-system/v2/` passes the grid sweep:
  `GRID_URL=http://localhost:3000 npm run test:grid`.
- Every value traces to a token, a named constant, or a data module.
- Count the client islands you added and the route JS you added; each section
  ships at most one island.

**Fix the cause, never suppress.** `eslint-disable`, `@ts-ignore`,
`@ts-expect-error`, and `@ts-nocheck` hide problems. The single sanctioned
disable is the hydration-safe portal-target `setState` (see
`50-react-effects-a11y.mdc`); it always carries the comment.

**Do not use `npm run build` as a pre-commit check.** It invalidates the dev
cache. `tsc --noEmit` + `lint` give the same signal. Use `next build` for
deploy verification and bundle-size checks only.

## The dev server

**Never start your own dev server.** The owner keeps `npm run dev` on port
3000; verify against `http://localhost:3000`. Two servers share one `.next`
directory and corrupt each other's chunks. If port 3000 is not answering, say
so and ask. Without `GRID_URL` the grid sweep spawns its own `next dev` on port
4823 and hits the same conflict — always pass `GRID_URL`.

## Don't hallucinate

- Never invent copy, data, or content. Every string comes from a data module,
  Figma, or an explicit instruction.
- Never invent a component API. Read the component before using a prop.
- Never assume a file or export exists. Verify by reading it.
- When unclear, stop and ask. Undoing a bad assumption costs more.

## Search before you build

- Icons and marks: `design-system/v2/icons.tsx` and `public/media/brand/`.
- Assets: `design-system/v2/media.ts` and `public/media/`.
- Tokens: `design-system/v2/tokens/` before writing any color, spacing, type,
  motion, or z value.
- Data helpers and route handlers: the `@keystone-sites/*` packages.
- Behaviour hooks: `data-action` contracts already wired (`open-chat`,
  `open-gallery`) before adding a new one.

The Figma MCP does not know the codebase; cross-reference its exports before
committing anything. It names assets with SHA hashes — rename to descriptive
kebab-case or delete the duplicate.

## Docs and comments

- Docs stay in sync with code, in the same change. If someone read only the
  docs and only the code, they must agree.
- Write docs minimal, one idea per sentence, no preamble, no code snippets
  unless prose cannot carry the rule. Document what exists, not plans.
- Comments explain *why*, never *what*. A useful comment carries a non-obvious
  reason, a constraint the code cannot express, or a trade-off that survived a
  discussion. Record a Figma node id and read date where a value came from a
  node. Never cite a document that does not exist; never write banner
  comments — split the file instead.
- User-facing strings use literal Unicode characters. The only escapes are
  invisible significant characters (`\u00a0`, `\u200d`, `\u202f`), commented.

## Git

- **Commit only when the human asks in that turn.** Passing checks is not
  permission. Leave the tree dirty and report what is ready.
- Branches: `feature/`, `fix/`, `docs/`, `chore/`. Messages follow Conventional
  Commits with an imperative summary.
- Messages are ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English: active voice, one idea
  per sentence, 20 words or fewer per sentence, plain approved words ("start",
  not "initiate"; "use", not "utilize"), no noun clusters over three words.
- One commit = one logical unit; the smallest complete one (one primitive, one
  section, one token layer). Related docs ride the same commit.
- `tsc` and `lint` pass before every commit. Never `git push --force` to
  `main`. PRs squash to a few logical commits.

## Never delete pages or routes

Pages, route handlers, and config files are removed only on explicit
instruction. Flag an apparently unused one with a comment. Internal refactors
that preserve observable behaviour are fine; the URL surface never disappears
silently.
